<!--{ cl01 : rich }-->
# 11. The command line

Stilo is also a command, `stilo`, which you have already used to start this
tutorial. It renders documents without the browser: for a script, a report
to print, or a check that a document has no errors. Here you use it on the
lessons you have just edited. About five minutes.
<!--{ cl02 : markdown }-->
## Go to your copy

Leave the terminal where the tutorial runs as it is, and open a second one.
Your copy of the tutorial is in this folder:

<% texl.import "~/project.texl" ~> (project -> "`" + project.data.tutorial_folder + "`") %>

Go there with `cd` followed by the folder, in double quotes:
`cd "…"`. Type `stilo --help` to see the commands, and
`stilo render --help` for the options of one.
<!--{ cl03 : rich }-->
## Render a document

`stilo render` prints a document with every formula replaced by its value.
Try it on the formulas lesson, with your answers in it:

```
stilo render 04-formulas.texl
```

The lines start with their markers, which stay in the output: they are
comments, which do not show when the output is read as Markdown or HTML. To
write the output to a file, add `-o` and a file name, as
`-o formulas.md`.
<!--{ cl04 : rich }-->
## Errors on the command line

The document [report](files/report.texl), in the folder `files`, has an
error. Render it:

```
stilo render files/report.texl
```

The floor area shows `[[Undefined]]`, and a last line says how many
undefined values there are. Add `--debug` to learn why: the output numbers
each error, and the report under it says where it was made (file, line and
column), what went wrong, and what caused it.

```
stilo render files/report.texl --debug
```

With `--strict`, the command fails (exit status 1) when the document has an
error, so a script can stop before it publishes a broken document. The output
is still written.
<!--{ cl05 : rich }-->
**Your turn.**

1. Open the [report](files/report.texl) in the browser, fix its error (the
   length is a text: remove its quotes) and save with **Ctrl+S**.
2. Render it again, strictly, into a Markdown file next to it:

```
stilo render --strict files/report.texl -o files/report.md
```

The check below reads `files/report.md` and shows ✓ Done when it exists
and has no undefined value.
<!--{ cl06 : formula }-->
<% texl.import "files/report.md" ~> (report -> tutorial.check ((type report.source == "Text") ? (text.find "[[Undefined" report.source < 0))) %>
<!--{ cl07 : rich }-->
The command line works on the files of the folder, the same files that the
browser edits: a document saved in the browser is ready for `stilo render`,
and a document changed by another program shows in the browser when you open
it again.

More in the manual: [The stilo command](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/index.html)
and [stilo render](https://www.onlabs.org/manual/cli/render.html).
<!--{ cl08 : rich }-->
